Why we built this)

“Today I’ll walk you through our secure, banking-grade digital signature flow.

The core problem we’re solving is:

How do we ensure that a transaction is genuinely initiated by the user
While also guaranteeing:
No tampering
No replay
Strong authentication binding
And end-to-end cryptographic integrity

Traditional approaches rely heavily on server-side trust or static credentials.
Our design shifts this to a cryptographically verifiable, per-transaction trust model.”

🧠 High-Level Approach

“What we’ve implemented is:

Per-transaction signing (not session-based trust)
Strong identity binding using JWT
Client-side cryptography using WebCrypto
Hybrid encryption for confidentiality
Replay protection using nonce + request state

So every transaction becomes:
👉 individually authenticated, signed, and verifiable”

🔁 Step 1: Initialization Flow

“Let’s start with the initialization phase.”

What happens:
Client sends:
payload
userId
sessionId
Server:
Validates session via Session Service
Determines authType dynamically (risk-based / policy-based)

👉 This is important:

Authentication is not static — it adapts (password / token / MFA)

Then we generate security primitives:
requestId → uniquely identifies transaction
nonce → prevents replay
Public keys:
Server key (from KMS)
Password encryption key
🔐 Binding Token (Critical Design)

“We create a bindingToken (JWT) which contains:

sessionId
requestId
userId
nonce
authType
expiry

And it is signed by the server.”

👉 Why this matters:

Prevents context tampering
Binds:
identity
session
transaction
authentication method

This is the anchor of trust in the entire system.

DB State

“We store:

requestId
nonce
payload hash (not raw payload)
sessionId
status = INIT”

👉 This enables:

replay protection
integrity validation
auditability
💻 Step 2: Client-Side Signing & Encryption

“This is where our design becomes significantly stronger than traditional systems.”

On the client:
Uses WebCrypto (non-exportable keys)
Generates ephemeral key pair per transaction

👉 This is critical:

Even if one transaction is compromised, others are safe.

What the client signs:

It signs:

payload hash
nonce
bindingToken hash
requestId
ephemeral public key

👉 This ensures:

Transaction cannot be reused
Signature cannot be replayed
Context cannot be altered
Encryption layers:
Password → encrypted with password public key
Payload bundle → encrypted using AES
AES key → encrypted using server KMS public key

👉 This gives:

Confidentiality (AES)
Secure key exchange (KMS)
Credential protection
🔍 Step 3: Validation Flow (Server Side)

“Now the server performs layered validation.”

✅ Step 1: Decryption
AES key decrypted via KMS
Payload decrypted
✅ Step 2: JWT Validation
Verify signature
Check expiry
Extract:
sessionId
requestId
nonce
authType

👉 Ensures:

Request context is authentic and untampered

✅ Step 3: Replay Protection
Match requestId + nonce with DB
Validate session again
Ensure status = INIT

👉 Guarantees:

One-time usage
No replay attacks
✅ Step 4: Payload Integrity
Recompute hash
Compare with stored hash

👉 Ensures:

Payload was not modified after initialization

✅ Step 5: Authentication (Before Signature Validation)

“This is a key design decision.”

We validate credentials first:

Password via Crypto Service
Token via RSA validator

👉 Why this order?

We ensure user identity is verified before trusting signature

✅ Step 6: Signature Validation

Now we verify:

Signature using ephemeral public key
Ensure signed data includes:
payload hash
nonce
bindingToken hash
requestId
ephemeral public key

👉 Guarantees:

User actually signed this transaction
Signature is bound to:
this session
this request
this payload
📊 State Transitions
INIT → SIGNED (success)
INIT → USED (failure / replay / invalid attempt)

👉 Prevents:

reuse
double execution
🧾 Audit & Logging

“We log only what is necessary and safe:”

requestId, sessionId, userId
payload hash (not raw payload)
authType
verification result

👉 This ensures:

audit compliance
no sensitive data leakage
🛡️ Security Guarantees (This is where you impress architects)

“This design provides:”

1. End-to-End Integrity
Payload hash validated at both ends
2. Strong Identity Binding
JWT ties user + session + request + authType
3. Replay Protection
Nonce + DB state + one-time usage
4. Forward Security
Ephemeral keys per transaction
5. Tamper Resistance
Signature includes full transaction context
6. Credential Protection
Password never sent in plaintext
7. Zero Trust Between Steps
Every layer re-validates independently
🚀 Why This Is Better Than Traditional Approaches

“Compared to typical systems:”

Traditional	Our Approach
Session-based trust	Transaction-level trust
Static keys	Ephemeral keys
Weak replay protection	Strong nonce + state enforcement
Credentials-only auth	Auth + cryptographic signature
Server-side signing	Client-side signing
